WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.435 --> 00:00:01.410
RETO MEIER: Today I
learned that when you're

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.940
visiting the city
by the bay, you

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:06.350
may want to avoid spending too
much time in the Tenderloin.

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:08.400
I'm Reto Meier, Google
Developer advocate.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.950
Join me today as I investigate
the San Francisco Police

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.080
Department incidents
public data set

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.870
to discover how crime is
distributed around the city

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:19.020
without the danger of having
to go outside with BigQuery.

00:00:23.326 --> 00:00:25.530
If you create a heat
map like this one,

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:27.720
showing the density
of SFPD incidents

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:31.140
in San Francisco since 2010,
the Tenderloin district

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:34.710
really stands out as a place
we probably want to avoid.

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:36.450
We can investigate
further by querying

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:38.340
for areas with a
high concentration

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:39.720
of a particular crime.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.150
By calculating the standard
deviation of both the latitude

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:44.150
and longitude for
each type of crime,

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:45.900
we can find which
crimes have the greatest

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:50.340
concentration in a particular
area and where those areas are.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.380
From this table, we
can see that drug crime

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.090
is the most concentrated.

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.270
In total, 75% of the
drug-related crimes

00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:58.000
in the categories from
the previous table

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.750
occurred in this part
of San Francisco.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:03.960
The red boundary here indicates
the Tenderloin district.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.910
We also learned that
prostitution arrests

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:07.320
are highly concentrated.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.090
In this case, they're
split between two

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:11.100
separate but distinct areas.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.890
Now by going in reverse, we
get this table showing us

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:15.750
the most highly
distributed crimes--

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:19.320
crimes that occur with similar
frequencies all over the city.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.600
Notice that burglary,
theft, and found property

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.820
make up 7 of the top 10.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:26.580
This map shows locations
with at least one burglary

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:27.855
or attempted break-in.

00:01:27.855 --> 00:01:30.030
In terms of
residential break-ins,

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:32.580
74% are the only
break-in recorded

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.770
at that particular location.

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:38.400
This map shows only locations
with five or more burglary

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:39.840
break-ins.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:41.730
In terms of personal
safety, it's

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:44.130
the areas where there's lots
of tourists and shoppers

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:46.800
where you're most likely to get
mugged or pick-pocketed while

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.080
in the city as shown
in this heat map.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:50.990
BigQuery includes
many more public data

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:53.380
sets for San Francisco
as well as other cities,

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:55.380
including New York and Chicago.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:57.930
What can you discover about
the cities we live in?

